1 Version 1.34.1
════════════════

  • Include Git recovery evidence in the decision model's request for
    supported single-file deletions. Ellama checks that the file's bytes
    and mode match both `HEAD' and the index. If the evidence no longer
    holds before the shell starts, Ellama stops the deletion.
  • Create committed and modified files for the paired deletion cases in
    the manual evaluation.


2 Version 1.34.0
════════════════

  • Add an optional decision model for irreversible tool calls. Set
    `ellama-tools-irreversible-decision-provider' to enable it. Ellama
    makes at most one request per eligible call, using the tool
    arguments and available file or SQL evidence. Ellama now requires
    `llm' 0.33.0 or later.
  • Check known effects locally, including direct file removals, hard
    Git resets, and shell output that would erase an existing file. For
    supported shell writes with several destinations, Ellama checks each
    destination and can settle the call without the model.
  • Verify file recovery using a matching Git version, a version written
    by the active session, or a backup that session created. Ellama
    rechecks the evidence before the write. With `srt' enabled, Git
    reads follow the same sandbox policy as the tool call.
  • Add `trash_files' for recoverable removals and `backup_file' for
    saving a copy before an edit. The trash backend is configurable, and
    refusals can point to the trash or a backup where appropriate.
  • Scan nested argument keys locally and withhold the decision request
    when a key contains a secret. An incomplete scan or a failed
    decision request follows the configured irreversible-action policy.
  • Add manual evaluations for irreversible verdicts and Git
    recovery. The suites run on demand and report results without
    executing fixture commands.


3 Version 1.33.2
════════════════

  • Fix file reads when a path uses ordinary spaces in place of
    nonbreaking spaces, as can happen with macOS screenshot names. If
    the exact path is missing, `read_file', `grep_in_file',
    `lines_range', and `count_lines' can use a unique match in the same
    directory.
  • Read `srt' settings as UTF-8 so sandbox rules can contain Unicode
    paths.


4 Version 1.33.1
════════════════

  • Fix `json-end-of-file' errors when displaying empty tool results or
    incomplete JSON text, allowing conversations to continue.


5 Version 1.33.0
════════════════

  • Add `session_file' tool to expose the current session transcript as
    a file, allowing history-aware subagents to read and recover past
    session context during execution.

  • Omit tool results from session compaction by default.  A new
    configurable opt-in (controlled by a dedicated custom) allows
    including tool results while keeping tool calls and arguments in the
    compaction input, keeping the compaction summary lean by default.

  • Integrate compacted tool-result recovery with the session
    transcript: tool calls and arguments are kept in the compaction
    summary so subagents can recover from compacted sessions, and
    transcript reads continue to obey session result truncation (SRT)
    policy.

  • Fix the compacted tool-result recovery assertion to match the new
    compaction behavior and add corresponding regression tests.

  • Fix the compaction hint docstring width so hints display correctly
    in the UI.

  • Extend `ellama-eval--instrument-tool-spec' to trace asynchronous
    tools whose first argument is a callback, and add a
    `balanced-edit-hooks' hypothesis profile that validates edits
    through a shell byte-compilation hook, surfaces hook failures to the
    agent, and records edit-hook failure and recovery counts in the
    evaluation summary.

  • Add `ellama-eval' configuration for lexical-binding declarations in
    eval fixture workspaces so all test fixtures run in lexical binding
    mode.

  • Add configuration to pin manual generation to Emacs 30.1 in CI, and
    let CI regenerate the committed manual on each build.


6 Version 1.32.3
════════════════

  • Use `start-file-process' for tool subprocesses so commands can run
    in remote TRAMP directories.


